L000104		\abst{
L000105			Commercially-relevant quantum computers will require large numbers of high-performing qubits that can be manufactured, integrated, and controlled at scale.
L000106			Silicon exchange-only (EO) qubits~\cite{
L000107				bacon_universal_2000,
L000108				divincenzo_universal_2000,
L000109				fong_universal_2011,
L000110				langrock_reset-if-leaked_2020,
L000111				andrews_quantifying_2019,
L000112				ha_flexible_2022,
L000113				weinstein_universal_2023,
L000114				ha_two-dimensional_2025,
L000115				sun_full-permutation_2024,
L000116				madzik_operating_2025}
L000117			are a strong candidate modality due to their control-signal simplicity and  compatibility with advanced semiconductor manufacturing~\cite{
L000118				zwerver_qubits_2022,
L000119				steinacker_industry-compatible_2025,
L000120				xue_cmos-based_2021},
L000121			but questions remain around the achievability of sufficiently low noise and a scalable control and wiring solution~\cite{
L000122				xue_cmos-based_2021,
L000123				patra_cryo-cmos_2018,
L000124				mehrpoo_benefits_2019,
L000125				geck_control_2019,
L000126				pauka_cryogenic_2021,
L000127				bartee_spin-qubit_2025,
L000128				kiene_134_2021}.
L000129			Here we introduce a quantum processing unit composed of a custom-designed cryogenic CMOS controller, a novel high-density superconducting ribbon cable, and a low-noise EO qubit device.
L000130			The quantum chip features a three-rail array of 54 exchange-coupled quantum dots, configurable to host up to 18 EO qubits.
L000131			We integrate and use these components to demonstrate qubit performance for both single-qubit and entangling operations that advances the EO state of the art~\cite{weinstein_universal_2023,ha_two-dimensional_2025,madzik_operating_2025} by an order of magnitude.
L000132			We further validate this system by implementing a distance-5 repetition code~\cite{google_exponential_2021} and a \mbox{distance-2} quantum error detecting code~\cite{
L000133				linke_fault-tolerant_2017,
L000134				dam_end--end_2024,
L000135				takita_IBMrep_2017,
L000136				zhang_422_2026,
L000137				reichardt_422_2025,
L000138				undseth_weight-four_2026}
L000139			then make detailed comparisons with simulations.
L000140			Our approach facilitates a utility-scale quantum computer with manageable operational and capital requirements.
L000141		}
L000142	
L000143		\maketitle
L000144	
L000145	
L000146	
L000147		From transistors to integrated circuits to Si CMOS, the dominant computing technologies of the modern era have been determined by advantage in cost-effective manufacturability.
L000148		Building a commercially-viable \emph{quantum} computer will demand the same---efficient fabrication not just of the qubit chip, but also of the systems responsible for control signal generation and delivery.
L000149		The rapid advancement of the scale and fidelity of Si spin qubits~\cite{
L000150			mills_two-qubit_2022,
L000151			stano_review_2022,
L000152			burkard_semiconductor_2023,
L000153			madzik_operating_2025,
L000154			steinacker_industry-compatible_2025,
L000155			wu_simultaneous_2025,
L000156			takeda_quantum_2022,
L000157			undseth_weight-four_2026},
L000158		driven in part by their compatibility with advanced semiconductor processes, has outpaced that of other system components~\cite{
L000159			xue_cmos-based_2021,
L000160			patra_cryo-cmos_2018,
L000161			mehrpoo_benefits_2019,
L000162			geck_control_2019,
L000163			pauka_cryogenic_2021,
L000164			bartee_spin-qubit_2025,
L000165			kiene_134_2021}.
L000166		In resolving this disconnect, a system architect must choose between placing control signal hardware at room temperature (burdened by the complexity of routing numerous high-fidelity analog links)~\cite{
L000167			mills_two-qubit_2022,
L000168			zwerver_qubits_2022,
L000169			steinacker_industry-compatible_2025,
L000170			madzik_operating_2025,
L000171			steinacker_industry-compatible_2025,
L000172			wu_simultaneous_2025,
L000173			takeda_quantum_2022,
L000174			undseth_weight-four_2026},
L000175		co-locating it with the mK qubits (with tyrannical cooling requirements)~\cite{bartee_spin-qubit_2025,jordan_quantum_2026},
L000176		or using an intermediate temperature~\cite{
L000177			xue_cmos-based_2021,
L000178			patra_cryo-cmos_2018,
L000179			mehrpoo_benefits_2019,
L000180			geck_control_2019,
L000181			franke_rents_2019,
L000182			pauka_cryogenic_2021,
L000183			tien_222_2026}.
L000184		This third option enjoys greater Carnot efficiency and avoids the room temperature ``wiring bottleneck,''~\cite{franke_rents_2019} but must transmit numerous signals to mK while maintaining thermal isolation.
L000185	
L000186		Our system follows the third option, using a fully-custom low-power CMOS chip operating at 4~K to generate all time-varying qubit control signals.
L000187		We resolve the thermal isolation problem by routing those signals through a high-density, low-thermal-conductance superconducting ribbon cable to the quantum chip at mK (\figsubref{qpufig}{a}).
L000188		As integrated, this ``quantum processing unit'' (QPU, photograph in \efigref{iqpu_ed} \ ) has modest requirements for its connections to room temperature: only digital communication, qubit readout, and static biases (\figsubref{qpufig}{b}).
L000189		The superconducting interconnect maintains excellent signal integrity and bandwidth while also serving as a thermal standoff, preventing the 4~K controller from unduly heating the mK stage.
L000190		EO qubits also pair well with power-constrained CMOS signal generation since their required control waveforms (shape-insensitive baseband voltage pulses) are similar to standard digital signals.
L000191		The qubit chip, ribbon, and controller are each fabricated with semiconductor wafer processes, a strong indicator for manufacturability.
L000192		The QPU is designed for the function that will most occupy any utility-scale quantum computer: error correction.
L000193		Below, we verify this capability with repeated syndrome measurements in {[}3,1,3{]} and {[}5,1,5{]} repetition codes as well as the {[}{[}4,2,2{]}{]} quantum error detecting code.
L000194	
L000195		\section{Quantum chip}\label{quantum-chip}
L000196	
L000197		Our qubit devices each feature 54 low-noise exchange-coupled quantum dots distributed over three rails (\figsubref{qpufig}{c}).
L000198		Qubits are encoded in the joint spin state of three individual electrons in three dots (see \hyperref[methods-section]{Methods}).
L000199		Electrons are vertically confined in a Si/SiGe quantum well which is engineered to increase valley splitting.
L000200		Si and Ge in the heterostructure are isotopically enhanced to reduce magnetic noise.
L000201		Lateral confinement is achieved using electrostatic gates patterned on top of the heterostructure through a proprietary 200-mm wafer foundry process.
L000202		The intrinsic charge noise of these devices is more than an order of magnitude lower than our previous Single-Layer Etch-Defined Gate Electrode (SLEDGE) technology~\cite{ha_flexible_2022,weinstein_universal_2023,ha_two-dimensional_2025}.
L000203		Additional device details are found in Methods.
L000204		Following fabrication, wafers are probed to select high-yielding die then diced and bump bonded to a fine-pitch laminate LGA package (\efigref{packagedchip} \ ).
L000205		Devices are installed on a daughterboard thermalized to the mixing chamber of a dilution refrigerator.
L000206		When fully integrated, we observe an average electron temperature of 150~mK (\firstSIlink{Environmental control}{environmental-control}).
L000207	
L000208	
L000209		\begin{figure*}[t]
L000210			\centering
L000211			\smartincludegraphics[width=\textwidth]{./media/fig_1}
L000212			\caption{
L000213				\label{qpufig}
L000214				\textbf{An integrated quantum processor.}
L000215				\textbf{a,}
L000216				The QPU is installed at the bottom of a commercial dilution refrigerator and connected to room temperature with flex cables and coaxial lines (\SIlink{Flex cable}{flex-cable}).
L000217				It is a tightly integrated system consisting of a controller motherboard at 4~K, qubit daughterboard at mK, and superconducting ribbon cable bridging the two.
L000218				Custom-designed transimpedance amplifiers (TIAs, \SIlink{4~K transimpedance amplifier}{transimpedance-amplifier}) are thermalized to 4~K but located below the daughterboard to minimize capacitance.
L000219				\textbf{b,}
L000220				The room temperature subsystem provides digital signaling and static current and voltage biases.
L000221				Qubit readout signals are also digitized at room temperature, though we anticipate future systems will incorporate that function inside the cryostat~\cite{kiene_134_2021}.
L000222				The ribbon cable transmits all 150 time-varying qubit control signals and 35 static device biases from 4~K to mK with minimal thermal load.
L000223				\textbf{c,}
L000224				Topology schematic of the quantum chip.
L000225				The 54 dots are arrayed in three sparsely-coupled rails and support up to 18 encoded qubits arrayed in a 3$\times$6 square lattice with nearest-neighbor coupling.
L000226				We flexibly partition connected trios of dots into qubits, motivated by yield, performance, or desired connectivity~\cite{ha_two-dimensional_2025}.
L000227				``P'' gates control dot occupancy and adjacent ``X'' and ``Y'' gates control the tunnel coupling between dots.
L000228				Dot loading, measurement, and qubit initialization utilize reservoirs accumulated by eight ``B'' gates coupled to the array by twelve ``T'' gates.
L000229				``M'' and ``Z'' gates form six dot charge sensors used for device tune-up and qubit state readout~\cite{blumoff_fast_2022}.
L000230				Various field gates deplete charge under inactive regions.
L000231				The biases applied to these electrodes are determined with a combination of manual and automated methods (\SIlink{Tune-up and calibration}{tune-up-and-calibration}).
L000232			}
L000233		\end{figure*}
L000234	
L000235		\section{Superconducting interconnect}\label{superconducting-interconnect}
L000236	
L000237		A high-density superconducting ribbon cable routes signals from the controller to the qubit chip.
L000238		The cable is fabricated from Nb on polyimide~\cite{tuckerman_flexible_2016,smith_improved_2024} using standard semiconductor wafer processes.
L000239		Though 296 coaxial signal lines are arranged in a single layer approximately 1 cm wide, crosstalk within the cable up to 10 GHz is limited to $<-80$~dB.
L000240		The ribbon maintains an effective thermal standoff due to its use of superconducting metal and small feature sizes, conducting less than 10~$\mu$W from the 4~K stage to the mixing chamber.
L000241		Laminate adapters at both ends allow the cable to be reversibly attached to both the daughterboard and motherboard (\efigref{cablephoto} \ ).
L000242		The round-trip electrical length from controller to qubit is roughly 3~ns.
L000243	
L000244		\section{Cryogenic CMOS controller}\label{cryogenic-cmos-controller}
L000245	
L000246		\begin{figure}[t]
L000247			\centering
L000248			\smartincludegraphics[width=\cwidth]{./media/fig_2}
L000249			\caption{
L000250				\label{swfig}
L000251				\textbf{The cryogenic CMOS controller.}
L000252				\textbf{a,}
L000253				The controller is a multi-component assembly: custom high-density capacitor arrays are bump-bonded to the CMOS die which is then integrated into a fine-pitch laminate LGA package (\efigref{controllerphoto} \ ).
L000254				The CMOS die comprises roughly 70 million transistors and has four distinct functional areas.
L000255				\textbf{b,}~The digital architecture is responsible for interpreting instructions and supplying every analog block with a pattern memory pointer every clock cycle.
L000256				\textbf{c,}~The gate driver layer is composed of an array of 78 nearly-identical analog blocks (one pictured) collectively comprising 156 output channels, 366 DACs, and 78 pulse generators (\SIlink{Cryo-CMOS qubit controller}{cryo-cmos-qubit-controller}).
L000257				The DACs (yellow, 0 to 1~V, RMS step size \textless{}~10~$\mu$V) are buffered by amplifiers (orange) with output capacitance provided by the capacitor arrays (purple).
L000258				Each exchange gate has a uniquely-associated pulse generator (blue) which emits timing signals (typical duration 400~ps to 6~ns, with approximately picosecond resolution).
L000259				To actuate symmetric exchange operation \cite{reed_reduced_2016}, these signals are distributed (green) to switch drivers (red) for the X gate and its neighboring P gates, generating synchronized voltage waveforms ($T_{10\%-90\%}\approx 150$~ps).
L000260				Each gate driver block has a local digital module to store configurable parameters such as output impedance and filtering.
L000261			}
L000262		\end{figure}
L000263	
L000264		The cryo-controller (\figsubref{swfig}{a}) is a mixed-signal system-on-chip fabricated in a commercial 130-nm RF CMOS process.
L000265		Circuit designs are optimized at the transistor level for performance at 4~K.
L000266		The controller has three primary functions: device tune-up, qubit state preparation and measurement (SPAM), and qubit state manipulation.
L000267		The first two functions are achieved by dynamically changing DAC output voltages and the third with fast, time-modulated, gate-synchronized switching between fixed DAC voltages.
L000268	
L000269		The controller's digital architecture (\figsubref{swfig}{b}) features an autonomous, multi-sequencer engine that operates at up to 250 MHz and uses a custom instruction set architecture for qubit control.
L000270		On-chip pseudo-random number generators enable memory-efficient characterization of qubit performance.
L000271		The controller consumes $\le$ 3.5~W in typical operation (\SIlink{Power consumption}{power-consumption}).
L000272		Pattern memory associated with each output stores parameters for voltages and pulse widths.
L000273		User programs are converted into machine code using a co-designed compiler and stored in a shared pool of 6144 words of instruction memory.
L000274		Every clock cycle, each of the sequencers interprets an instruction and updates a pattern memory pointer specifying analog behavior to user-assigned output blocks (\figsubref{swfig}{c}).
L000275		Once initialized, the program runs without further communication with room temperature.
L000276		The digital architecture uses a 52-line parallel bus and a Serial Peripheral Interface (SPI) for configuration, diagnostics, and memory readback.
L000277	
L000278		\section{Qubit performance}\label{qubit-performance}
L000279	
L000280		\begin{figure}[h]
L000281			\centering
L000282			\smartincludegraphics[width=\cwidth]{./media/fig_3}
L000283			\caption{
L000284				\label{performancefig}
L000285				\textbf{Qubit performance.}
L000286				\textbf{a,}
L000287				Empirical cumulative distribution functions (ECDF) for gate error of cryo-controlled EO qubits.
L000288				Mean errors of 2\e{-4} for single-qubit gates (blue) and 3\e{-3} for CNOT (orange) are indicated by vertical bars.
L000289				Single-qubit gates, each composed of 4 exchange pulses, are characterized with blind randomized benchmarking (BRB)~\cite{andrews_quantifying_2019}. CNOT sequences~\cite{divincenzo_universal_2000,fong_universal_2011,weinstein_universal_2023}, composed of 37 pulses in 45 timesteps, are characterized with an interleaved BRB variant (\SIlink{Blind randomized benchmarking (BRB)}{blind-randomized-benchmarking-brb}).
L000290				The lowest reproducible CNOT error was 9\e{-4}.
L000291				The dashed line is the fidelity of CNOT gates with simultaneous dynamical decoupling applied to all other qubits; this degradation is consistent with their slightly slower pulse cadence.
L000292				\textbf{b,}~Representative
L000293				charge noise data acquired using a cryoMUX system from a single device similar to that used in panel a.
L000294				Vertical lines indicate the median (674) and quartile (394 and 977) number of exchange oscillations before 1/$e$ amplitude decay in an experiment sweeping evolution duration at constant exchange energy, $N_{\text{osc}}$~\cite{reed_reduced_2016}.
L000295				Orange vertical text along the quartiles represent the simulated error contribution to CNOT error due to those values of charge noise.
L000296				\textbf{c,} Magnetic noise data taken from unique dot pairings on the same device in the same cryoMUX system.
L000297				Vertical lines indicate median (19.3~$\mu$s) and quartile (11.1~$\mu$s and 25.6~$\mu$s) decay envelope of a singlet decay experiment, \(T_{2}^{*}\)~\cite{kerckhoff_magnetic_2021}.
L000298				Simulated contributions to CNOT error are again annotated at quartile values.
L000299			}
L000300		\end{figure}
L000301	
L000302		Verifying gate performance is a principal consideration as it strongly influences the overhead required for quantum error correction (QEC).
L000303		The QPU used for the [[4,2,2]] experiment exhibits single-qubit and CNOT performance metrics (\figsubref{performancefig}{a}) that improve on the prior state of the art by an order of magnitude~\cite{ha_two-dimensional_2025,madzik_operating_2025,weinstein_universal_2023}.
L000304		For budgeting purposes, we characterize noise intrinsic to the qubit chips using a purpose-designed ``cryoMUX'' system.
L000305		Those systems utilize a custom cryogenic demultiplexer (MUX), similar in functionality but different in operation to those in Refs.~\onlinecite{puddy_multiplexed_2015,paquelet_wuetz_multiplexed_2020,pauka_characterizing_2020,eastoe_method_2025,schmidt_135_2025,thomas_rapid_2025}, that is paired with room-temperature electronics and are capable of controlling a configurable subset of nine dots.
L000306		In \figsubref{performancefig}{b} and c, we show charge and magnetic noise data from a qubit chip of the same type as those used for the multiqubit experiments.
L000307		The measured intrinsic charge noise also improves on the prior state of the art by approximately a factor of ten~\cite{ha_two-dimensional_2025,
L000308			madzik_operating_2025}.
L000309		Mean values of intrinsic device charge and magnetic noise would collectively contribute only 0.02\% to absolute CNOT error.
L000310		Instead, modeling and additional system characterization indicate that ``extrinsic'' terms such as static magnetic field gradients and contextual pulse miscalibration dominate observed error.
L000311	
L000312	
L000313		\section{Repetition codes}\label{repetition-codes}
L000314	
L000315		High-fidelity one- and two-qubit operations are necessary but not sufficient for effective computation---we must also show that performance does not degrade as circuit complexity grows.
L000316		We first validate the multiqubit system performance by executing ``classical'' repetition codes.
L000317		Repetition codes are of interest because they enable qubit-efficient study of low-probability correlated errors~\cite{google_exponential_2021} which may unacceptably degrade QEC performance.
L000318		We realize a distance-5 repetition code (simplified circuit illustrated in \figsubref{mqfig}{a}, \SIlink{[5,1,5] demonstration}{demonstration-515}) using seven EO qubits.
L000319		Critically, this circuit applies first-order ``NZ1'' full-permutation dynamical decoupling~\cite{sun_full-permutation_2024} to all idling qubits during all SPAM, two-qubit, and leakage-reduction operations.
L000320		The circuit also employs parallel state preparation, measurement, and exchange operations~\cite{madzik_operating_2025}. Comparing the distance-5 logical performance (LER $\approx 5\e{-3}$) to the average performance of distance-3 subsets assessed from the same underlying data, we observe a scaling factor $\Lambda_{5/3} = 4.7$ (\figsubref{mqfig}{c}).
L000321		Data from this experiment show as-yet unexplained fluctuations in error rate which were not observed in either the distance-3 or [[4,2,2]] data discussed below (\SIlink{Spikes in detection events}{spikes-in-detection-events}).
L000322	
L000323		\begin{figure*}
L000324			\smartincludegraphics[width=\textwidth]{./media/fig_4}
L000325			\caption{
L000326				\label{mqfig}
L000327				\textbf{Multiqubit validation results.}
L000328				\textbf{a,}
L000329				Simplified circuit for the distance-5 repetition code.
L000330				Classical information is encoded in five data qubits which are repeatedly interrogated for bit flips using two ancilla qubits.
L000331				Each syndrome extraction (SE) round is broken into two waves and ancillae are re-used.
L000332				A single instance of the 200-round experiment consists of 1,805 initializations, 805 measurements, and 335,422 exchange pulses.
L000333				\textbf{b,}~The
L000334				leakage reduction units (LRUs) used here are composed of an ancilla initialization and a Reset-If-Leaked (RIL) control sequence~\cite{langrock_reset-if-leaked_2020} consisting of 24 or 21 exchange pulses in 30 timesteps (24-pulse variant depicted here).
L000335				Each angle in the figure represents a partial swap between neighboring spins that is actuated by corresponding controller-generated voltage pulses.
L000336				\textbf{c,}~We
L000337				sweep the number of syndrome extraction rounds per shot in the {[}5,1,5{]} sequence quasi-logarithmically from 1 to 200 and repeat each 50,000 times.
L000338				The syndrome data, interpreted with a naïve parity check decoder, indicate a logical error rate (LER) of 5.0$\e{-3}$ for the distance 5 code (purple) and 2.3\e{-2} for the average of distance-3 subsets (red), yielding a distance improvement factor \(\Lambda_{5/3} = 4.7\).
L000339				\textbf{d,}~The
L000340				detector event fraction of a fidelity-optimized {[}3,1,3{]} experiment with (green) and without (gray) LRUs included in each syndrome extraction wave show the effectiveness of LRUs at preventing the build-up of leakage.
L000341				The LER of this instantiation of the distance-3 code was 3.2\e{-3} when LRUs were included.
L000342				\textbf{e,}~[[4,2,2]]-code
L000343				logical fidelity (defined as the projection of the post-selected state onto the expected state) and throughput after three rounds of SE are comparable for all four input states.
L000344				Spin- and event-level simulations show relatively good model-hardware agreement (\SIlink{Multiqubit experiments}{multiqubit-experiments}).
L000345			}
L000346		\end{figure*}
L000347	
L000348		The three-spin encoding of this qubit minimizes any distinction between bit- or phase-flips in repetition codes but introduces the important factor of spin-state leakage out of the computational subspace~\cite{
L000349			divincenzo_universal_2000,
L000350			fong_universal_2011,
L000351			andrews_quantifying_2019,
L000352			langrock_reset-if-leaked_2020,
L000353			weinstein_universal_2023}.
L000354		We mitigate that concern in all multiqubit circuits shown here with the addition of LRUs (\figsubref{mqfig}{b}).
L000355		These gadgets conditionally reset data qubits if and only if they have leaked out of the computational space.
L000356		We confirm the effectiveness of this mitigation with a distance-3 repetition code experiment using a selection of qubits optimized for {[}3,1,3{]} performance (\SIlink{[3,1,3] demonstration}{demonstration-313}).
L000357		As expected, we observe that the inclusion of LRUs in the syndrome extraction (SE) circuit leads to a stable detector event fraction (DEF), but their omission leads to leakage accumulation and a DEF that grows with round (\figsubref{mqfig}{d}).
L000358		This stability is likely a prerequisite for effective QEC.
L000359	
L000360		We improve confidence in our error model by exhaustively comparing the {[}3,1,3{]} results to theoretical predictions.
L000361		In that case, a qubit-level event simulator (calibrated with measured gate error rates from Interleaved BRB experiments performed in-situ and assignment fidelity measurements for SPAM) agreed with the experimental LER to 15\% relative inaccuracy.
L000362		We found similarly good agreement using a spin-level simulator calibrated on measured dot-level noise terms ($N_{\text{osc}}, T_{2}^{*}$ static field gradients, etc.) and assignment fidelity.
L000363		An ersatz miscalibration term (chosen to match simulated and measured CNOT BIRB error) was added to reflect errors arising from deterministic imperfections in signal generation and transmission.
L000364		In this simulation, that term is responsible for approximately 80\% of detection events.
L000365		The agreement between these two simulators and experiment is an encouraging indication that our system exhibits emergent Markovianity and is well-described by a simple and scalable performance model.
L000366		More discussion of that measurement, the associated simulations, and error attribution can be found in the SI sections ``\hyperref[simulation]{Simulation}" and  ``\hyperref[multiqubit-experiments]{Multiqubit experiments}."
L000367	
L000368		\section{Quantum error detection}\label{quantum-error-detection}
L000369	
L000370		We perform a final system validation by implementing a quantum error detecting (QED) code that is potentially sensitive to error types not detected by classical codes.
L000371		The [[4,2,2]] code, previously demonstrated in trapped ion qubits~\cite{linke_fault-tolerant_2017,dam_end--end_2024}, superconducting qubits~\cite{takita_IBMrep_2017}, and neutral atom qubits~\cite{reichardt_422_2025} embeds two logical qubits into four physical qubits and enables the detection of any single-qubit (weight-1) error.
L000372		Similar syndrome measurements~\cite{undseth_weight-four_2026} and logical operation~\cite{zhang_422_2026} were recently demonstrated in silicon qubits.
L000373		Each round of syndrome extraction entails two weight-4 measurements (\(XXXX\) and \(ZZZZ\)), interleaved flag qubit measurements, and leakage reduction.
L000374	
L000375		We implement this code with six physical qubits on a system similar to, but slightly improved from, that of the {[}5,1,5{]} experiment (\SIlink{{[}{[}4,2,2{]}{]} demonstration}{demonstration-422}).
L000376		During syndrome extraction, we compile all single-qubit gates into composite two-qubit operations.
L000377		We prepare the data qubits into each of four different initial states $\ket{0000}, \ket{0110}, \ket{{+}{+}{+}{+}},$ and $\ket{{-}{-}{+}{+}}$ and perform three rounds of syndrome extraction before measuring their final states.
L000378		The first round of syndrome measurements predictably projects these into different logical states ($\ket{\bar{0}\bar{0}}, \ket{\bar{1}\bar{1}}, \ket{\bar{+}\bar{+}},$ and $\ket{\bar{-}\bar{+}}$, respectively).
L000379		When preparing in the $X$-basis we switch the ordering of two stabilizer measurements to project into a logical state more quickly.
L000380	
L000381		We assess code performance by post-selecting on all error-detecting measurements: syndrome results, flag measurements, and a last syndrome computed from the final data qubit measurements.
L000382		This process ultimately rejects roughly 77\% of the 10,000 experimental shots acquired per preparation basis.
L000383		We then analyze the final measurements and assess a two-logical-qubit state fidelity after three rounds, finding $F_L=0.95$ largely independent of the initial state (\figsubref{mqfig}{e}).
L000384		If we instead ignore the error-detecting measurements, we find an average $F_L=0.59$, indicating the effectiveness of QED.
L000385	
L000386		To examine correlations between error events, we turn to detector-error-model (DEM) analysis~\cite{chen_decoders_2022,arms_estimating_2026,blume-kohout_estimating_2025}.
L000387		As shown in \hyperref[dem-analysis-422]{SI}, the DEM analysis of the empirical data and of spin simulations show no statistically-relevant unexpected events in the [[4,2,2]] experiment after appropriate grouping of events with the same weight.
L000388		This indication of ``well-behaved'' errors is encouraging for larger-scale QEC.
L000389	
L000390		\section{Towards commercial relevance}\label{towards-commercial-relevance}
L000391	
L000392		Our demonstration marks a milestone in the technological maturity of semiconductor spin qubits.
L000393		Relative to prior reporting on these qubits \cite{weinstein_universal_2023,madzik_operating_2025}, we have shown transformative improvements in operational fidelity and power- and space-efficient cryogenic control.
L000394		Conventional concerns such as charge noise, valley splitting, and leakage out of the computational space are no longer limiting.
L000395		Rather, the quality of our demonstration is set by how well each of the system sub-components are made to work together as a whole.
L000396		Development now shifts to integration improvements such as magnetic hygiene, power delivery, internal signal integrity, and device calibration, all having highly feasible engineering solutions.
L000397		Our ability to predict system-level performance based only on local measures like gate error and noise parametrics also indicates that the fundamental design decisions of the technology are sound.
L000398	
L000399		We believe that a system design integrating 4~K cryogenic control, low-noise mK EO qubits, and superconducting interconnection provides a practical foundation for a utility-scale fault-tolerant quantum computer in a single, commercial cryostat.
L000400		To get there, work still remains in maturing both the components and their integration.
L000401		Necessary advances include a manufacturable interconnect and qubit back-end routing solution, controllers with even lower power consumption per qubit, and improved device uniformity to reduce tune-up overhead.
L000402		A system-level quantum architecture tailored to the connectivity, noise environment, and other properties of EO qubits must also be elaborated.
L000403		Taken together, the scale and cost of a system anticipated by the prototype reported here position semiconductor spin qubits as a leading technology to realize commercially-relevant quantum computing.
