L000676		\section{Methods}\label{methods-section}
L000677	
L000678		\subsection{Qubit Chip}\label{qubit-chip}
L000679	
L000680		SiGe heterostructures for the qubit chip were grown on 200~mm silicon wafers by chemical vapor deposition (CVD).
L000681		A strain-relaxed SiGe buffer layer was grown on top of a Si wafer~\cite{deelman_metamorphic_2016}, terminating with a SiGe layer that matches the Ge composition of the SiGe barriers to the quantum well.
L000682		The SiGe surface of the buffer received chemical mechanical polishing to planarize the surface before CVD growth of the quantum well and barriers.
L000683		The barrier layers are Si\textsubscript{$1-x$}Ge\textsubscript{$x$} grown using SiH\textsubscript{4} and GeH\textsubscript{4} with alloy compositions in the range $x=0.25$ to $0.35$.
L000684		The SiGe heterostructure, enriched with $^{28}$Si and depleted of $^{73}$Ge, was engineered to increase valley splitting energy~\cite{
L000685			zhang_genetic_2013,
L000686			marcks_valley_2025,
L000687			mcjunkin_sige_2022,
L000688			stehouwer_engineering_2025}.
L000689	
L000690		Qubit chips were fabricated on the SiGe heterostructure wafers described above, leveraging CMOS-compatible process integration.
L000691		This combines the proprietary, qubit-specific front-end-of-line integration with industry-standard back-end-of-line interconnect integration.
L000692		Ohmic contacts and electrically inactive regions were defined by optical lithography and ion implantation of P and Ar, respectively.
L000693		The qubit gate design, with a minimum feature pitch of 70 nm, was patterned with electron-beam lithography.
L000694		Gate contacts and routing were formed using a self-aligned dual damascene process with a total of three minimum-pitch routing levels.
L000695		At each level, designs for vias and for routing are pattern-transferred into a hard mask using electron beam lithography.
L000696		Dry etching opened vias and routing trenches within an SiO\textsubscript{2} interlayer dielectric.
L000697		CVD W metallization, with a TiN liner, filled the vias and trenches which were subsequently isolated using chemical mechanical planarization.
L000698	
L000699		Next, three additional larger-pitch metal layers were formed to contact the W layers and fan out the routing lines to the pad pitch.
L000700		These three layers were formed in a co-planar waveguide stack, with each layer patterned by optical lithography.
L000701		Each layer was formed with a single damascene via made of W followed by a routing or ground plane layer composed of Nb using a subtractive integration.
L000702		Al bond pads were formed to contact the Nb routing and allow for wafer probing of the qubit devices.
L000703		Following wafer probing, In bumps were added to the Al pads and the wafers were diced to singulate each qubit chip for later flip-chip packaging.
L000704	
L000705		\subsection{Exchange-only qubits}\label{exchange-only-qubits}
L000706	
L000707		Exchange-only (EO)~\cite{
L000708			bacon_universal_2000,
L000709			divincenzo_universal_2000,
L000710			fong_universal_2011,
L000711			langrock_reset-if-leaked_2020,
L000712			madzik_operating_2025,
L000713			ha_flexible_2022,
L000714			weinstein_universal_2023,
L000715			ha_two-dimensional_2025,
L000716			andrews_quantifying_2019,
L000717			sun_full-permutation_2024}
L000718		means that the only physical interactions employed on electrons trapped in quantum dots are
L000719		(1) initialization into antisymmetric spin-singlet states, i.e. $\ket{\uparrow\downarrow}-\ket{\downarrow\uparrow}$,
L000720		(2) execution of partial spin swaps $U(\theta) = \cos\theta + i \sin\theta\times\text{SWAP}$, where SWAP acts between a pair of spins, and
L000721		(3) pairwise measurement of singlet vs. triplet (i.e. $\ket{\uparrow\uparrow}, \ket{\uparrow\downarrow}+\ket{\downarrow\uparrow}, \ket{\downarrow\downarrow}$.
L000722		All such interactions are available from direct-current (DC) or ``baseband'' pulsing using the Pauli-spin blockade mechanism, which uses electrode voltages that push electrons closer or further from another.
L000723		The resulting ``exchange interaction'' reduces the energy of the singlet state relative to all triplet states due to a combination of Coulomb repulsion and the Pauli-exclusion principle.
L000724		No magnetic fields of any kind are required, and the entire spin-system, barring errors, would maintain total spherical symmetry.
L000725	
L000726		Qubits are formed from three spins in three dots using the symmetry-respecting encoding of a decoherence free subsystem~\cite{bacon_universal_2000,
L000727			divincenzo_universal_2000,
L000728			fong_universal_2011,
L000729			langrock_reset-if-leaked_2020}.
L000730		The eight resultant spin states may be described in the basis denoted as \(|S_{12},S;m\rangle\) where \emph{S}\textsubscript{12} encodes the total spin angular momentum of the first two spins (\emph{S}\textsubscript{12}=0, singlet; \emph{S}\textsubscript{12}=1, triplet), \emph{S} encodes the total spin of all three spins, and \emph{m} encodes the spin projection.
L000731		Ideal exchange-only initialization, logic, and read-out address only on the \emph{S}\textsubscript{12} degree of freedom, and \emph{S} would remain, for all encoded qubits, with constant value \emph{S}=1/2.
L000732		The projection m=±1/2 is a random and inconsequential gauge degree of freedom.
L000733		Global magnetic fields \emph{B} impart global phases which are also inconsequential.
L000734		The angles $\theta$ for each exchange ``gate'' $U(\theta)$, which we call exchange angles or ``exchangles'', are determined by the time integral of the exchange energy, and as such are calibrated against DC voltage pulses with no direct dependence on pulse shape.
L000735		Accordingly, the control of this qubit can be realized by (precise) switching between pairs of voltage levels---highly reminiscent of digital logic---and so is amenable to the energy-efficient cryocontrol system we have described.
L000736	
L000737		The dominant qubit errors can be categorized into two categories: (1) miscalibration of exchangles $\theta$, or (2) dot-level gradients of the magnetic field.
L000738		In multiqubit operation, \emph{both} error types cause some fraction of encoded error (i.e. bit-flips and phase flips on the \emph{S}\textsubscript{12} number) and some fraction of leakage error (i.e. flips from \emph{S}=1/2 to \emph{S}=3/2).
L000739		Quantitative modeling of these contributions, as well as the translation of physical processes into exchangle miscalibrations and magnetic gradients, are discussed in the SI section ``\hyperref[simulation]{Simulation}".
L000740		Exchange miscalibration can be incoherent (i.e. dynamically fluctuating charge noise from the qubit chip, interconnect, or controller) or coherent (i.e. static or contextual miscalibration from imperfect signal generation or transmission); the last of these dominates in the demonstrations presented.
L000741		Similarly, gradients of the magnetic field can arise from fluctuating hyperfine interactions with \textsuperscript{29}Si and \textsuperscript{73}Ge nuclear spins or from static magnetic screening and flux-trapping arising from local superconductivity.
L000742		The former source of gradients is mitigated using isotopic enhancement, the limitations of which determine the $T_2^*$ values of approximately 20 $\mu$s (\figsubref{performancefig}{c}).
L000743	
L000744		The latter source is mitigated by limiting the magnetic field of the device to be close to zero, for which we use current coils external to the fridge to vector-cancel the Earth's magnetic field.
L000745		As such, all experiments described in this paper are at approximately zero magnetic field.
L000746		Dephasing due to residual magnetic gradients of both types is reduced for idling qubits in our demonstrations using permutational dynamical decoupling.
L000747		This permutes sets of three spins using SWAP operations $U(\pi)$ in a way which causes local gradients across triple-dots to average to global fields impacting only the irrelevant gauge number \emph{m}~\cite{sun_full-permutation_2024}.
