L001290	
L001291	The cryo-controller can source signals for up to 54 quantum dot (``P'') gates, 66 exchange (``X'' and ``Y'') gates, 12 tunnel (``T'') gates, and 24 gates used for initialization and readout (``Z'', ``M'', and ``S'').
L001292	There is a 1:1 mapping of 150 of 156 outputs to the physical qubit gates they control which are naturally arranged into ``sectors'' labeled A-F.
L001293	A sector is the set of gates used to form 3 physical qubits (PQB) vertically aligned in 3 rails of the device (9 P-gates, 11 X/Y-gates, 2 T-gates, and 4 SPAM gates per sector).
L001294	To bias quiescent gates such as bath and field gates (not shown in \figref{figS2}), low noise voltages are routed directly to the qubit device from room temperature electronics.
L001295	Three exchange gate outputs in Sector F (XF3, XF6, and XF9) are unused by the qubit and routed to diagnostic connectors on the motherboard, and 3 of 6 available S-gate outputs are not connected.
L001296	The chip also includes two spare gate drivers routed out of the system for diagnostics or characterization.
L001297	The controller only requires power, a handful of DC analog biases, and a manageable number of intermediate-speed lines for digital communication to be provided from room temperature.
L001298	
L001299	\subsection{Digital architecture}\label{digital-architecture}
L001300	
L001301	The cryo-controller's digital architecture consists of six independent instruction sequencers, a shared bank of instruction memory (6144 words, each 32 bits wide), and, for each gate driver output, a custom ``pattern memory'' register that store up to 512 commands that are each 24 bits wide.
L001302	The controller's digital interface consists of 66 signals: a 2-wire differential system clock, a 52-line front-side parallel bus (including write clock, address, and data), an industry-standard 4-wire serial peripheral interface (SPI), and 8 auxiliary inputs and outputs.
L001303	The front-side bus transmits program data to instruction and pattern memories using the write clock and executes at the operating frequency of the system clock.
L001304	The SPI is used for reading and writing a variety of configuration registers within the digital architecture and analog gate driver layer.
L001305	The auxiliary I/O includes signals that may be used to interface with external readout electronics, inputs for real-time user-selectable instruction branching (``Triggers''), outputs for general purpose use (``Markers''), and specific digital diagnostic outputs (``Digital Test Outputs'').
L001306	Any of the digital input signals can be multiplexed to these diagnostic outputs to be routed to room temperature.
L001307	This provides the capability to perform in-situ loop-back signal connectivity testing within the dilution refrigerator.
L001308	
L001309	The multi-sequencer core is responsible for providing a memory address to each pattern memory cell every clock cycle.
L001310	The addresses sent to pattern memory are computed according to a customized qubit-control instruction set architecture (ISA).
L001311	The ISA includes instructions for stepping, pausing, jumping, looping, branching, memory stack manipulation, toggling of external outputs, and more.
L001312	Each sequencer has integrated pseudorandom number generators that can be accessed with specialized instructions for memory-efficient randomized benchmarking (see \secref{blind-randomized-benchmarking-brb}).
L001313	There is also the capability to enforce inter-sequencer coordination using real-time interrupts and interrupt resolution logic.
L001314	
L001315	Addresses computed by the sequencers are used to retrieve pattern memory words which are routed to each analog driver every clock cycle.
L001316	Pattern memory words specify information such as DAC voltage, pulse duration, and other analog functionality that is decoded and executed by local digital modules within the gate driver blocks themselves.
L001317	It is possible to configure the mapping of any pattern memory cell (and therefore any analog driver) to receive its address pointer from any sequencer using the SPI.
L001318	This functionality provides an enormous degree of flexibility in assigning independent or coordinated control to arbitrary parts of the qubit device.
L001319	This may be advantageous in particular for performing experiments on the qubit array in the presence of physical defects.
L001320	Collectively, the cryo-controller's digital architecture is capable of autonomously executing effectively arbitrary-length randomized benchmarking sequences and complex multi-qubit experiments.
L001321	
L001322	The cryo-controller contains several on-chip pseudorandom number generators.
L001323	The output of each is a deterministic function of a user-defined seed.
L001324	Each random number generator is a maximum length linear feedback shift register (LFSR) modified to shift out \emph{M} pseudorandom bits in a single clock cycle.
L001325	On-chip logic divides the set of \emph{2\textsuperscript{M}} possible bit-vector values into \emph{K} equal sized subdivisions and selects the subdivision \emph{R} that contains the random bit-vector.
L001326	The resulting random integer \emph{R} is uniformly distributed between \emph{0} and \emph{K-1}.
L001327	
L001328	We implement randomized benchmarking (RB) with on-chip instructions that jump to an address + offset where the offset is the value of an on-chip random number.
L001329	For 1-qubit RB we compile each of the 24 Clifford gates into subroutines with the first instruction of each subroutine located in a single software-defined jump table.
L001330	The first instruction of each subroutine jumps out of the table, and the final instruction of each subroutine is the instruction to jump to address + offset where the address is the start of the jump table and the offset is a random number between 0 and 23.
L001331	Additionally, we load an on-chip register with the desired RB sequence length and decrement this register on completion of each Clifford gate.
L001332	When this register is decremented to zero, the jump to address + offset behavior is modified to jump to the first address past the jump table where we load the first instruction for the inverse Clifford gate subroutine.
L001333	Calculation of the inverse Clifford gate is determined during program compilation by simulating the pseudorandom number generation process using the same seed value.
L001334	We execute more complex RB protocols (2-qubit RB, interleaved RB, etc.) with a similar procedure utilizing multiple different sized random numbers and software-defined jump tables (see \secref{random-clifford-sampling}).
L001335	
L001336	\subsection{Analog gate driver layer}\label{analog-gate-driver-layer}
L001337	
L001338	
L001339	\begin{figure*}
L001340		\centering
L001341		\smartincludegraphics[width=165mm]{./media/fig_s3}
L001342		\caption{
L001343			\textbf{Block diagram of an analog gate driver sector and the variations of gate driver blocks within it.}
L001344			The gate driver layer contains six sectors.
L001345		}
L001346		\label{figS3}
L001347	\end{figure*}
L001348	
L001349	The ``sector'' is a natural way to organize both the qubit and the cryo-controller's analog gate driver layer.
L001350	A block diagram showing this organization for the controller in detail is shown in \figref{figS3}.
L001351	The fundamental analog driver building block is called the ``DNX'' dual gate driver which controls one P, M, Z, or S-gate and one X, Y, or T-gate.
L001352	There are 3 variants of this building block (``D6X'', ``D5X'', and ``D3X'') where ``N" equals the number of DACs in the block, determined by the number of unique high-precision voltage levels required to control the gate pair.
L001353	The ``X'' in the name denotes an exchange pulse generator.
L001354	To control a sector of qubit gates, 13 DNX blocks are integrated together along with driver biasing circuitry.
L001355	The gate driver sector is replicated 6 times to form the cryo-controller's full analog layer consisting of 78 DNX blocks containing a total of 366 DACs and 78 pulse generators.
L001356	
L001357	Device tune-up and SPAM are performed by changing DAC output voltages.
L001358	The default DAC bandwidth is about 10~MHz, which balances SPAM switching speed ($T$\textsubscript{10\%-90\%} ≈ 35 ns) and output noise for exchange.
L001359	DACs can dynamically change their switching speed in order to generate both accelerated ($T$\textsubscript{10\%-90\%} ≈ 10~ns) transitions as well as slow, pseudo-linear ramps with tightly-controlled slew rates intended for traversing charge transitions (the ``tie bar'') during qubit initialization.
L001360	
L001361	GHz-bandwidth, time-modulated exchange gate pulses are generated for qubit state manipulation by switching between fixed, buffered DAC values.
L001362	The precise timing information comes from the pulse generator, a high-resolution digital-to-time converter (DTC).
L001363	The exchange gate driver's local digital module receives a digital input word from pattern memory and converts a portion of it into a variable-width, logic-level pulse by the DTC.
L001364	The logic-level pulse is translated into an output pulse between the appropriate voltage levels with a high-speed output switch and associated switch driver.
L001365	Each exchange gate driver's pulse generator output is distributed to neighboring dot gate switch drivers with low timing skew to form time-aligned, triplicate pulse waveforms for symmetric exchange operation \cite{s_reed_reduced_2016}.
L001366	
L001367	Qubit gates act like open circuits (i.e. are high impedance), so any pulse sent from the cryo-controller is sent back as a near-perfect reflection that must be sufficiently attenuated before the next pulse is launched.
L001368	Failure to do so results in inter-symbol interference (ISI) error.
L001369	To this end, the gate driver's output impedance is designed to match the nominal 50~Ω characteristic impedance of the interconnect in the system.
L001370	The output switch features configurable resistance control to tune the termination to 50~±~1~Ω using the SPI.
L001371	The parasitic capacitance of the output is minimized to provide at least 20~dB return loss up to the 500~MHz analog bandwidth of instrumentation.
L001372	The output switch also features a configurable switched-capacitor output pulse filter to help mitigate reflections.
L001373	An adjustable capacitor is connected to the output for the duration of a pulse to add to its rise and fall times by up to 400~ps, and it is disconnected before the reflected pulse arrives to avoid degrading the quality of the output termination.
L001374	
L001375	DACs are buffered with unity-gain voltage amplifiers that provide approximately 60~MHz bandwidth for restoring the exchange and compensation pulse voltages to their calibrated values in between pulses in a sequence.
L001376	The buffer amplifier is designed in conjunction with a sizable off-chip load capacitor that provides the initial charge needed to form high-speed pulse rising and falling edges.
L001377	The buffer limits voltage droop on the pulse amplitude for the duration of the pulse and restores the charge on the output capacitor for the next pulse.
L001378	These charge reservoir and stabilization capacitors are fabricated as custom, high-density flip-chip capacitor arrays that are bonded directly to the CMOS die to minimize parasitic inductance.
L001379	Flip-chip capacitors that are not mapped to buffer amplifier outputs are connected to the analog power supply rails to lower the impedance and increase the effectiveness of the power delivery network (PDN).
L001380	
L001381	\begin{figure}
L001382		\centering
L001383		\smartincludegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{./media/fig_s4}
L001384		\caption{
L001385			\textbf{Illustrations of how the analog gate driver \textbf{a,} executes tune-up and SPAM, and \textbf{b,} exchange pulsing operations.}
L001386		}
