L001566	\section{Environmental control}\label{environmental-control}
L001567	
L001568	\subsection{Thermal management}\label{thermal-management}
L001569	
L001570	High-fidelity state preparation and measurement of our exchange-only spin qubit processor requires operation at ultra-low cryogenic temperatures.
L001571	To accomplish this, we utilize a commercial Bluefors XLD1000 dilution refrigerator, capable of rejecting 1~mW of heat at 100~mK.
L001572	Cooling power at 4~K is provided by three Cryomech PT-425 pulse tube coolers: one dedicated for pre-cooling the circulating \textsuperscript{3}He/\textsuperscript{4}He mixture and two providing a nominal 5~W of cooling power to the 4~K plate of the cryostat.
L001573	This 4-Kelvin cooling capacity supports the heat loads from cabling as well as the cryo-controller (\secref{power-consumption}).
L001574	The CMOS-generated control signals are routed to the qubit chip via a 296-trace superconducting Nb/Polyimide ribbon cable (\secref{superconducting-interconnect}) that launches off a printed circuit board (PCB) which houses the cryo-controller at the 4~K stage and lands on a separate PCB that houses the qubit chip at the mK stage.
L001575	
L001576	The signals to support the qubit chip and cryo-controller are binned into several functional roles, outlined in Fig.~1 of the main text, each having their own electrical requirements.
L001577	Digital and analog power rails for the cryo-controller, as well as readout amplifier power, are delivered with individual coaxial cables to minimize IR drops and quiescent power dissipation to the 4~K stage.
L001578	The signals supporting the cryo-controller digital I/O and reference voltages (along with a select set of DC qubit chip biases) are routed from room temperature to the 4~K stage of the cryostat via two chains of custom, 100-trace flexible cryogenic cables (see  \secref{flex-cable}).
L001579	The thermal loads at the 50~K and 4~K stages of the dilution refrigerator are minimized by appropriate choice of material according to the requisite signal bandwidth: DC references and qubit chip static biases are routed through a higher resistance CuNi/CuNi (signal/ground) cable chain with 10~MHz of bandwidth, while the high-speed digital I/O is routed through a hybrid Cu/CuNi-Cu/Cu cable chain with a combined bandwidth of 450~MHz.
L001580	We estimate the total conductive heat load to 4~K from all room temperature signals to be less than 200~mW, consistent with measured data shown in \figref{figS14}a.
L001581	
L001582	The analog and digital power dissipation of the cryo-controller itself dominates the parasitic loads discussed above, and is estimated at \textless3.5~W (\secref{power-consumption}).
L001583	Close proximity of the cryo-controller to the qubit chip in our architecture improves signal integrity but also necessitates effective thermal isolation of the two components.
L001584	In order to avoid thermal gradients across the motherboard exceeding the superconducting critical temperature (8.5~K) of the mK interconnect, the chip die is directly heat sunk to the 4~K plate through a copper framework.
L001585	Based on sweeps of directly applied 4~K heat while monitoring interconnect trace resistance, we estimate approximately 6~W of cooling power available for cryo-control at 4~K in the current design.
L001586	The combined heat leak to the qubit chip from amplifier cabling and the Nb interconnect is measured to be less than 20~$\mu$W, as shown in \figref{figS14}a, enabling mixing chamber temperatures as low as \textless20~mK in a fully integrated system.
