L001802	\subsection{GAQQMap}\label{gaqqmap}
L001803	
L001804	Considering the notation \(|S_{12},S;m\rangle\) described in the \hyperref[methods-section]{Methods section}, recall that encoded qubits live in the manifold $S=1/2$ and $m=\pm 1/2$.
L001805	We refer to these four states (two gauge states for each of two qubit states) as $D$, referring to the gauge ``Doublet.''
L001806	However, errors cause leakage into \emph{S}=3/2 and its Quadruplet of $m$ values, a manifold we call $Q$.
L001807	In the presence of arbitrary errors, relative populations and coherences of all eight states could in principle be important, but the lack of spin polarization at small magnetic field renders a symmetry enabling us to neglect the sign of $m$. We therefore model a gate as acting on states maximally mixed in sign($m$), and (in simulation as well as in physical measurement) ignore (trace over) this degree of freedom after each operation.
L001808	With only this binary degree of freedom traced, our resulting state space for each triple-dot is the ququart of states $\ket{S_{12},S;|m|}  = \ket{0,1/2;1/2}, \ket{1,1/2;1/2}, \ket{1,3/2;1/2},$ and $\ket{1,3/2;3/2},$ abbreviated as $\ket{0\textsubscript{D}}, \ket{1\textsubscript{D}}, \ket{0\textsubscript{Q}},$ and $\ket{1\textsubscript{Q}},$ respectively.
L001809	Hence this is a two-dimensional encoded (\emph{D}) qubit and a two-dimensional leakage (\emph{Q}) space.
L001810	This averaged manifold is referred to as a Gauge-Averaged QuQuart (GAQQ), and logical gates, with or without error, can be described by a map between double-Pauli operators on these states, which we call a GAQQMap.
L001811	We may represent the map in the Kraus operator-sum representation, \(\mathcal{E}(\rho) = \sum_{i}^{}{K_{i}\rho K_{i}^{\dagger}}\) where \(K_{i}\) are the Kraus operators corresponding to each traced gauge state sign($m$).
L001812	The Kraus operators then intuitively represent the dependence on sign($m$).
L001813	While non-physical, for visualization of our generated sequences, we compute the mean of the Kraus operators of the GAQQMap and organize them by the encoded and leakage spaces of each qubit.
L001814	See \figref{figS19}\ for examples of this technique applied to CNOT and XCNOT two-qubit gates.
L001815	For modeling gate error via simulation, which we discuss further in \secref{qubit-characterization}, GAQQMaps including simulated errors from full spin dynamics are created and used to populate event-based simulations.
