L002124	\subsubsection{BRB implementation and analysis}\label{brb-implementation-and-analysis}
L002125	
L002126	The \emph{N}-qubit BRB experiment proceeds as follows:
L002127	
L002128	\begin{enumerate}
L002129	\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
L002130	\item
L002131	\emph{Initialize the system in}
L002132	\(\left.
L002133	\ |0 \right\rangle^{\otimes N}\)
L002134	\item
L002135	\emph{Apply $n$ random 2-qubit Clifford operations,}
L002136	\(C_{1},\ C_{2},\ \ldots C_{n}\)
L002137	\item
L002138	\emph{Choose a target Clifford} \(\ts{C}{tot}\) \emph{from among all
L002139		combinations of single-qubit identity or Pauli-$X$ operations.
L002140		E.g.
L002141		a
L002142		one-qubit} \(\ts{C}{tot}\) \emph{could be either $I$ or $X$, while a
L002143		two-qubit} \(\ts{C}{tot}\) \emph{could be any of $II, IX, XI,$ or $XX$.}
L002144	\item
L002145	\emph{Apply an inverting Clifford} \(\ts{C}{inv}\)\emph{, chosen such
L002146		that} \(\ts{C}{inv} \cdot C_{n}\ldots \cdot C_{2} \cdot C_{1} = \ts{C}{tot}\)
L002147	\item
L002148	\emph{Measure the final singlet population for each qubit}
L002149	\item
L002150	\emph{Repeat across many different choices of random Clifford
L002151		realization} \(C_{i}\)\emph{, overall Clifford} \(\ts{C}{tot}\)\emph{, and
L002152		Clifford count $n$}.
L002153		\end{enumerate}
L002154	
L002155		With no error, \(\ts{P}{singlet} = 1\) for the \(\ts{C}{tot} = I^{\otimes N}\) branch, and 0 for all other branches.
L002156		In the limit of large encoded-space error, the system approaches a fully depolarized state, and \(\ts{P}{singlet} \rightarrow \frac{1}{d}\) for all branches, where \(d = 2^{N}\) is the dimensionality of the qubit space.
L002157		By contrast, any leaked DFS qubit measures as a triplet, so in the limit of large \emph{leakage} error \(\ts{P}{singlet} \rightarrow 0\).
L002158	
L002159		To analyze BRB data, we assume that the system undergoes two exponential decays as a function of \emph{n}: one within the encoded space, and another into a larger, partially leaked space.
L002160		This is only strictly accurate for a single qubit with a sufficiently strong external B field, but numeric and empirical evidence suggests that it is a good approximation for a wide range of one- and two-qubit error models.
L002161		Under this approximation and neglecting SPAM errors, the probability \(P_{I}\) of measuring all singlets in the \(I^{\otimes N}\) branch is
L002162	
L002163		\begin{equation}
L002164	P_{I} = \frac{1 - L}{d} + \frac{d - 1}{d}(1 - p)^{n} + \frac{L}{d}(1 - q)^{n},
L002165	\end{equation}
L002166	
L002167	where \emph{p} and \emph{q} are the encoded and leakage decay rates, respectively, and \emph{L} is the asymptotic leakage probability.
L002168	The remaining \(d - 1\) branches---collectively termed the ``X-type'' branches---behave very similarly to each other, starting at \(\ts{P}{singlet} = 0\) and decaying up to some asymptote \(\leq {1}/{d}\).
L002169	It is convenient to consider the mean singlet probability \(P_{X}\) of all such branches:
L002170	
L002171	\begin{equation}
L002172	P_{X} = \frac{1 - L}{d} - \frac{1}{d}(1 - p)^{n} + \frac{L}{d}(1 - q)^{n},
L002173	\end{equation}
L002174	
L002175	The sum and difference of these terms can be fit to single-exponential ansatz:
L002176	
L002177	\begin{equation}\label{eq:birb_fit1}
L002178	P_{I} - P_{X} = 2B(1 - p)^{n},
L002179	\end{equation}
L002180	\begin{equation}\label{eq:birb_fit2}
L002181	\frac{1}{d}P_{I} + \frac{d - 1}{d}P_{X} = A + C(1 - q)^{n}.
L002182	\end{equation}
L002183	
L002184	Finally, we derive error rates accounting for SPAM error following the algebra in \cite{s_andrews_quantifying_2019}:
L002185	
L002186	\begin{align}
L002187	\Gamma &= \frac{C}{2B}q, \\
L002188	\varepsilon &= \frac{d - 1}{d}p + \frac{1}{d}\Gamma.
L002189	\end{align}
L002190	
L002191	\subsubsection{Interleaved BRB}\label{interleaved-brb}
L002192	
L002193	A BRB experiment on \emph{N} given qubits tells us the average \(\Gamma\) and \(\varepsilon\) over all possible Cliffords on those qubits.
L002194	To characterize a specific Clifford of interest \emph{U}, we modify the BRB experiment to interleave \emph{U} after each random Clifford, making the full sequence \([C_{1},U,C_{2},U,\ \ldots C_{n},U,\ts{C}{inv}]\).
L002195	If errors on \emph{U} are not strongly correlated with those on the random Cliffords, then the errors will add incoherently in the interleaved BIRB experiment, and we can approximate the errors due to \emph{U} alone by subtraction:
L002196	
L002197	\begin{equation}
L002198	\varepsilon_{U} \approx \ts\varepsilon{BIRB} - \ts\varepsilon{BRB},
L002199	\end{equation}
L002200	and likewise for \(\Gamma\).
L002201	
L002202	CNOT and XCNOT are Clifford operations with error rates comparable to the random Clifford error rate, so they are usually well-suited to characterization via interleaved BRB (although \figref{figS32} shows a striking counterexample).
L002203	RIL, by contrast, is not a Clifford gate or even an operation confined to the computational space: with general two-qubit inputs (including ancilla), it can produce leaked states.
L002204	However, if the ancilla qubit is guaranteed to be in singlet \(\ket{0}\), RIL acts as an identity on any (unleaked) data qubit state.
L002205	The process of initializing the ancilla and then applying RIL thus forms a non-unitary, leakage-reducing gadget that can be interleaved into \emph{one}-qubit RB, with random Cliffords applied to the data qubit.
L002206	
L002207	Finally, although SWAP is also a critical component of our experiment sequences, its error rate is much lower than the random Clifford error rate, and its periodic structure can lead to destructive interference with random Clifford error channels.
L002208	This means that BRB provides unreliable measurements of SWAP errors.
L002209	While SWAP error is included in our spin-level simulations and therefore in our error budgeting for multiqubit circuits, we do not rely on experimental interleaved BRB measurements of SWAP error for model calibration.
L002210	
L002211	\subsubsection{Random Clifford sampling}\label{random-clifford-sampling}
L002212	
L002213	To efficiently represent long BRB sequences in limited cryo-controller memory, we rely on the on-chip PRNG to sample random Cliffords, as described in \secref{digital-architecture}.
L002214	For one qubit BRB, we supply the controller with unique sequence definitions for all 24 one-qubit Cliffords and randomly choose among them.
L002215	For two qubits, we add definitions for SWAP and an entangler (e.g. CNOT), then construct uniformly random two-qubit Cliffords with the following algorithm:
L002216	
L002217	\begin{enumerate}
L002218	\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
L002219	\item
L002220	\emph{With probability 0.5, apply a SWAP.}
L002221	\item
L002222	\emph{Apply random one-qubit Cliffords to both qubits.}
L002223	\item
L002224	\emph{With probability 0.9, apply the entangler.}
L002225	\item
L002226	\emph{If the entangler was applied in step 3, apply random one-qubit
L002227		Cliffords to both qubits.}
L002228		\end{enumerate}
L002229	
L002230		For some datasets we have applied the entangler with unit probability (rather than 90\%), which results in an overly pessimistic estimate for two-qubit Clifford error.
