L002259	\subsection{Coherent spin simulation}\label{coherent-spin-simulation}
L002260	
L002261	Our qubit architecture can be modeled as a collection of spins subjected to magnetic fields and interacting with each other via the exchange interaction.
L002262	This is described by the following Hamiltonian:
L002263	
L002264	\begin{equation}\label{eq:pyquest_hamiltonian}
L002265	H(t) = \ \sum_{j}^{}{\gamma_{j}{\vec{B}_{j}}(t) \cdot {\vec{S}}_{j}} + \sum_{\left\langle j,k \right\rangle}^{}{J_{jk}(t){\vec{S}}_{j} \cdot {\vec{S}}_{k}}.
L002266	\end{equation}
L002267	
L002268	Here, \(\gamma_{j}\) is the gyromagnetic ratio for the electron in dot \(j\), \({\vec{S}}_{j}\, = \,{\sigma}_{j}\text{/}2\) the spin operator for the electron in dot \(i\), and \(\left\langle j,k \right\rangle\) denotes nearest neighbors.
L002269	The magnetic fields \({\vec{B}}_{j}(t)\) for each dot are a sum of the global applied magnetic field, any static gradients present in the device, along with fluctuating effective fields of magnitudes consistent with those originating from hyperfine interactions with stray spinful nuclei \cite{s_kerckhoff_magnetic_2021}.
L002270	The time-dependent exchange couplings \(J_{jk}(t)\) encode both the applied pulses (which we approximate as square pulses), as well as fluctuations due to charge noise.
L002271	
L002272	We solve the time-dependent Schrödinger equation under the Hamiltonian in Eq. \ref{eq:pyquest_hamiltonian} using a custom software package called the Python Quantum Exchange Simulator Tool Suite (pyquest).
L002273	Simulations of our qubit architecture are run in pyquest following these steps:
L002274	
L002275	\begin{enumerate}
L002276	\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
L002277	\item
L002278	The user passes a \emph{resource topology}, which provides the device graph, whose nodes are the spins and whose edges are the possible exchange couplings.
L002279	The topology also may or may not contain an \emph{encoding}, which describes how spins are grouped to form DFS qubits.
L002280	We use the encoding for GAQQMap (\secref{gaqqmap}) to track spin labels belong to DFS, but in general circuit simulation we only rely on the collection of spin and their connectivity.
L002281	\item
L002282	The user passes a \emph{flatseq} object, which is a data structure that contains the sequence of pulse operations and state preparation and measurement (SPAM), operations to apply to the spins.
L002283	
L002284	\begin{enumerate}
L002285		\def\labelenumii{\alph{enumii}.}
L002286		\item
L002287		This is typically encoded as a matrix, where each row is a timestep, each column a particular exchange axis, and each entry either zero or the desired pulse angle.
L002288		\item
L002289		A non-zero pulse exchangle \(\theta\) for an axis between \(i\) and \(j\) gives the time-integral of the exchange coupling \(J_{ij}(t)\) over the duration of the pulse.
L002290		Explicitly, we have: \emph{\hfill\break}\[\theta = \int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}{J_{ij}\left( t' \right) dt' }.\]
L002291		The start and end times \(t_{2}\) and \(t_{1}\) for each time step are inferred from user-supplied data.
L002292	
L002293		\item
L002294		State preparation (i.e., flushing to a singlet) and measurement are indicated by an additional list of flags specifying at which timesteps the operation(s) occur, and on which exchange axis.
L002295	\end{enumerate}
L002296	
L002297	\item
L002298	The user specifies noise parameters for the system, such as \(\ts{N}{osc}\) for each axis or \(T_{2}^{*}\) for each dot, the (static) magnetic field experienced by each spin, and static pulse miscalibration.  Note that in
L002299	pyquest, \(T_{2}^{*}\) is a property of each \emph{dot}, rather than each \emph{exchange axis}.
L002300	For a DFS qubit with three spins
L002301	\(\left\{ z,t,n \right\}\), with pairwise \(T_{2}^{*}\) values
L002302	\(\left\{ T_{2}^{*}(z,t),T_{2}^{*}\ (z,n),T_{2}^{*}\ (t,n)\  \right\}\),
L002303	the effective \(T_{2}^{*}\) inputted to simulation for all three spins is
L002304	\[T_{2}^{*}\ \  = \ \left\lbrack \frac{1}{3}\left( \frac{1}{{T_{2}^{*}\ (z,t)}^{2}} + \frac{1}{{T_{2}^{*}\ (z,n)}^{2}} + \frac{1}{{T_{2}^{*}\ (t,n)}^{2}} \right) \right\rbrack^{- 1/2}.\]
L002305	\item
L002306	Pyquest then generates a \emph{noise trajectory} (i.e., a particular sampling of fluctuating magnetic fields or fluctuating exchange couplings) and simulates application of the sequence with the sampled noise trajectory.
L002307	This process can be repeated for a desired number of shots, with pyquest automatically re-sampling noise for each shot.
L002308	\end{enumerate}
L002309	
L002310	Fluctuating magnetic fields at each dot, along with fluctuating exchange couplings, are generated via the Voss algorithm \cite{s_voss_efficient_2022}.
L002311	When dealing with time-dependent $({\vec{B}}_{j})$ or \(J_{jk}\), we Trotterize the applied operators; e.g., for a single-spin magnetic field term we do:
L002312	
L002313	\begin{equation}\begin{split}
L002314	\mathcal{T}\left\{ \exp\left( - \frac{i\gamma_{j}}{\hslash}\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}{{\vec{B}}_{j}\left( t' \right)} \cdot {\vec{S}}_{j}\,\text{d}t' \right) \right\}\ \  \mapsto \\ \ \ \prod_{n = 0}^{N}{\exp\left\lbrack - \frac{i\gamma_{j}}{\hslash}{\vec{B}}_{j}\left( t_{1} + n\frac{t_{2} - t_{1}}{N} \right) \cdot {\vec{S}}_{j}\frac{t_{2} - t_{1}}{N} \right\rbrack},
L002315	\end{split}\end{equation}
L002316	
L002317	where \(\mathcal{T}\) is the time-ordering operator, \(N = \left\lceil \left( t_{2} - t_{1} \right)\text{/}\tau \right\rceil\), and \(\tau\) is the splitting time.
L002318	Exchange, i.e. \(\ts{N}{osc}\), noise is Trotterized similarly.
L002319	For the simulations presented in this manuscript, we take the splitting time to be \(\tau = \ T_{2}^{*}\ \text{/}20\), where \(T_{2}^{*}\) is the \emph{shortest} \(T_{2}^{*}\) across all spins involved in the operator being Trotterized.
L002320	
L002321	Pyquest has two different backends for performing simulations: one using dense state vector methods, and the other using tensor network methods (more specifically, matrix product state methods).
L002322	We provide high-level overviews of these simulation backends below.
L002323	
L002324	\subsubsection{Spin state vector (SSV)}\label{spin-state-vector-ssv}
L002325	
L002326	The spin state vector (SSV) backend stores the state of the \(N\)-spin system in a dense state vector with \(2^{N}\) entries.
L002327	State vectors in the computational basis are mapped on to indices in the state vector in the standard way: namely, the state
L002328	\(\left| \sigma_{0}\sigma_{1}\ldots\sigma_{N - 1} \right\rangle\), with \(\sigma_{i} \in \text{\{}0,1\text{\}}\), corresponds to index \(\sum_{j = 0}^{N - 1}{\sigma_{N - 1 - j}2^{j}}\) in the state vector (see, e.g., Ref.~\cite{s_lin_exact_1993} for details).
L002329	
L002330	Single-spin magnetic field terms on dot $j$, $\vec{B}_{j} \cdot \sigma_{j}$, and exchange terms $\vec{S}_{j} \cdot \vec{S}_{k}$, are constructed respectively as $2 \times 2$ and $4 \times 4$ matrices in Pauli basis.
L002331	
L002332	
L002333	
L002334	
L002335	
L002336	
L002337	
L002338	
L002339	
L002340	
L002341	
L002342	
L002343	
L002344	Single-spin terms are exponentiated directly using \(\exp\left( i\vec{B} \cdot \sigma \right) = \cos\left( \left| \vec{B} \right| \right)I + i\sin\left( \left| \vec{B} \right| \right)\vec{B} \cdot \sigma \text{/}\left| \vec{B} \right|\), where \(\vec{B}\) includes the stepwise integrated field(s) (after Trotterizing as described above).
L002345	Matrices for multi-spin terms (e.g. exchange with fields or multiple overlapping exchanges) are either directly diagonalized and exponentiated (if involving three spins or fewer) or Trotterized to consecutive single or two-site terms (if involving more than three spins).
L002346	These operators are then applied to the state vector by iterating over all possible configurations of the spins not involved in the operation.
L002347	
L002348	To implement a SPAM operation on a given spin pair, we first construct the projectors onto the four possible angular momentum states \(\left\{ S,T_{0},T_{+},T_{-} \right\}\) for the pair in question.
L002349	In the computational basis, these read:
L002350	
L002351	\begin{align}
L002352		P_{S} &= \frac{1}{2}\ \begin{pmatrix}
L002353			0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
L002354			0 & 1 & - 1 & 0 \\
L002355			0 & - 1 & 1 & 0 \\
L002356			0 & 0 & 0 & 0
L002357		\end{pmatrix},&
L002358		P_{T_{+}} &= \ \begin{pmatrix}
L002359			1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
L002360			0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
L002361			0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
L002362			0 & 0 & 0 & 0
L002363		\end{pmatrix},&
L002364		\notag\\
L002365		P_{T_{0}} &= \frac{1}{2}\ \begin{pmatrix}
L002366			0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
L002367			0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\
L002368			0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\
L002369			0 & 0 & 0 & 0
L002370		\end{pmatrix},&
L002371		\ P_{T_{-}} &= \ \begin{pmatrix}
L002372			0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
L002373			0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
L002374			0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
L002375			0 & 0 & 0 & 1
L002376		\end{pmatrix}.
L002377	\end{align}
L002378	
L002379	Next, the probabilities \(p_{i} = \ \left\langle \psi \middle| P_{i} \middle| \psi \right\rangle\), for \(i \in \left\{ S,T_{0},T_{+},T_{-} \right\}\), of each angular momentum state are computed.
L002380	Then one outcome is sampled (weighted by the probabilities \(p_{i}\)) and the appropriate projection operator is applied.
L002381	For measurement, we apply either \(P_{S}\) (if the sampled result is \(S\)) or \(P_{T_{0}} + P_{T_{+}} + P_{T_{+}}\) (if the result is one of the triplet states) to the state and re-normalize.
L002382	In either case, the measurement result is recorded.
L002383	For initialization, we project to the same state that was sampled, and then apply a correction:
L002384	
L002385	\begin{itemize}
L002386		\item
L002387		For \(T_{0}\), we apply a rotation by \(\pi\) about the \(z\) axis, \(\exp\left( - i\pi\sigma_{z}\text{/}2 \right)\), to the first spin.
L002388		\item
L002389		For \(T_{+}\) and \(T_{-}\), we apply a rotation by \(\pi\) about the \(x\) axis, \(\exp\left( - i\pi\sigma_{x}\text{/}2 \right)\), to the first spin, and then apply the operator \(P_{S}\) and re-normalize.
L002390	\end{itemize}
L002391	
L002392	In either case, the result of these corrections is that the system is in the singlet (\(S\)) state; the purpose of doing initialization in this manner is to correctly simulate the back-action of the initialization on the rest of the system.
L002393	For initialization, we can also simulate errors via an affine transformation of the probabilities
L002394	
L002395	\begin{equation}
L002396		\begin{pmatrix}
L002397			p_{S} \\
L002398			p_{T_{0}} \\
L002399			p_{T_{+}} \\
L002400			p_{T_{-}}
L002401		\end{pmatrix} \mapsto A\begin{pmatrix}
L002402			p_{S} \\
L002403			p_{T_{0}} \\
L002404			p_{T_{+}} \\
L002405			p_{T_{-}}
L002406		\end{pmatrix} + \,\left| B \right\rangle,
L002407	\end{equation}
L002408	
L002409	with \(A\) a \(4 \times 4\) matrix and \(B\) a 4-element vector parameterizing the initialization error.
L002410	The outputted state can then be sampled from these new probabilities, and constructed by reversing the corrections performed above.
L002411	Measurement errors can be simulated after the fact by probabilistically altering the recorded measurement results.
L002412	
L002413	The advantage of SSV is that, when there are no overlapping exchange operators applied at the same time, all the operators applied to the state are constant size (\(2 \times 2\) or \(4 \times 4\)).
L002414	Applying gates or SPAM operations to the state is then \(\mathcal{O}\left( 2^{N} \right)\) in both space and time, rather than \(\mathcal{O}\left( 4^{N} \right)\) when constructing the full matrices.
L002415	The looping over configurations of uninvolved spins can also be done very efficiently using bit-twiddling techniques and pointer arithmetic.
L002416	Consequently, the SSV backend has very good performance for small (\(\lesssim 7\) DFS qubits, i.e. \(< 21\) spins) systems.
L002417	This is particularly advantageous for simulating one and two qubit RB, or single and two qubit gates.
