L002436	\subsubsection{Matrix product state (MPS)}\label{matrix-product-state-mps}
L002437	
L002438	The matrix product state (MPS) backend stores the state of the \(N\)-spin system as an MPS with a maximal bond dimension \(\chi_{\max}\).
L002439	Each spin can be assigned to a single MPS site, or multiple spins can be grouped onto a single MPS site.
L002440	Operators for single and multi-spin terms are constructed analogously as in SSV and applied to the \emph{physical} indices of the MPS.
L002441	Singular value decompositions (SVDs) are then performed as needed, along with truncation of the bond dimension.
L002442	SPAM operations are implemented analogously as in SSV.
L002443	
L002444	We use a modified version of the open-source quimb package \cite{s_gray_quimb_2018} which implements methods for tensor network operations (including contractions).
L002445	Importantly, this package represents the evolution under \(H(t)\) for the whole experiment as a tensor network, which allows the software to efficiently contract certain types of operations \cite{s_gray_hyper_2021}.
L002446	For example, consecutive operations occurring on the same MPS site (e.g., consecutive single-spin terms on the same spin, or exchanges acting on a pair of spins that are grouped in the same MPS site), and even operations occurring on neighboring MPS sites, can be contracted into a single operator.  See \figref{figS34} for an example.
L002447	This latter case is important, as fusing together multiple operations between the same pair(s) of MPS sites can reduce the number of SVDs required and improve performance.
L002448	
L002449	Our MPS simulation backend can also be run on one or more GPUs via a user-passed argument.
L002450	This is achieved by using the autoray \cite{s_gray_autoray_2026} library to dispatch tensor operations to the appropriate backend.
L002451	To run tensor operations on GPUs, we use cupy \cite{s_okuta_cupy_2017} as the backend.
L002452	For large systems and/or deep networks, this allows for significant improvement in simulation wall time; indeed, the simulations of larger circuits ({[}5,1,5{]} and {[}{[}4,2,2{]}{]}) presented in this manuscript make use of this feature.
L002453	The network contraction described above is a vital feature when running the MPS backend on GPUs, as it ensures the network does not exceed the amount of memory available to the GPU.
L002454	
L002455	Due to the overhead involved with storing the state as an MPS and contracting the tensor network, MPS is generally slower than SSV for systems with \(\lesssim 7\) DFS qubits.
L002456	Above this scale, the memory savings from truncating the bond dimension (\(\chi_{\max}\)) leads to better performance relative to SSV (especially when run on a GPU).
L002457	Our MPS simulator tracks the norms of the MPS after SVD truncation, allowing us to assess the reliability of the MPS approximation at a given \(\chi_{\max}\).
L002458	A crucial consideration when setting up an MPS simulation is how to group the spins into MPS sites: putting more spins onto each MPS site can reduce the number of required SVDs, at the cost of larger MPS matrices (for the same max bond dimension).
L002459	We find empirically that grouping the three spins of each DFS qubit into their own MPS site yields good performance, and seems to optimize (or nearly optimize) this tradeoff.
L002460	We use this grouping for the simulations presented in this manuscript.
L002461	
