L002561	\subsection{Event simulation}\label{event-simulation}
L002562	
L002563	Coherent simulation of each electron captures many microscopic noise phenomena, but the computational cost of the simulation grows exponentially with the number of spins.
L002564	For this reason, we turn to qubit-level event simulation, which is a common approach for evaluating quantum circuits in the presence of noise-induced errors.
L002565	All sources of microscopic noise are combined into an effective probability of a single encoded qubit error (explained in the GAQQmap procedure in \secref{constructing-qubit-operations}), and the quantum device is simulated as a series of expected (logical) and unexpected (noise) gates.
L002566	
L002567	Our internal software package which handles event simulation of circuits is called Squeaky.
L002568	Squeaky simulates quantum programs acting on DFS qubits made of three spins, and relies on an abstract syntax tree (AST) data structure.
L002569	To more reliably simulate our exchange-only qubit architecture, leakage out of the encoded subspace is tracked by a leakage bit assigned to each qubit.
L002570	When a leakage error occurs, this leakage bit is flipped to true, and the leakage is then propagated through the circuit according to the leakage properties of the applied gates.
L002571	Leakage is only removed in this simulator when a qubit is reset to the encoded \(|0\rangle\) state.
L002572	
L002573	Noise is injected into an ideal circuit via the circuit's AST representation, which enables efficient calculation of when potential faults can occur and what their error distributions are.
L002574	After this analysis, potential faults are inserted into the tree.
L002575	Figure \ref{figS39} provides an example of a simple program before and after noise injection.
L002576	After this injection of potential faults, one then samples the circuit many times (usually in parallel, with use of MPI), stochastically replacing potential faults with realized faults before each shot.
L002577	
