L002667	\subsubsection{Qubit orientations}\label{qubit-orientations}
L002668	
L002669	
L002670	
L002671	Exchange-only qubits are initialized by preparing a two-electron singlet and a third, uninitialized spin on three consecutive quantum dots.
L002672	The exchange ``axes'' of a single EO qubit, corresponding to a pair of spins undergoing exchange, are referred to by the geometric axes which would result from a qubit Bloch sphere on the EO qubit.
L002673	(We emphasize that this Bloch sphere is not that corresponding to the total spin of the EO qubit; that total-spin Bloch sphere would track the irrelevant gauge-degree of freedom).
L002674	For the EO qubit, the \emph{Z} axis refers to the pair of spins over which the singlet is initially prepared.
L002675	The \emph{N} axis refers to the axis between the middle spin and the initially uninitialized third spin.
L002676	Quantum angular momentum recoupling rules fix this axis is $N = Z\cos(\phi) + X\sin(\phi)$, with $\phi=2π/3$.
L002677	We also refer to $N$ and $Z$ as Bloch-sphere $\pi$ pulses about their corresponding axes, which in turn corresponds to complete swaps of corresponding spin pairs.
L002678	These axis names also correspond, for any given EO qubit, with two exchange gates; for example XA5XA6 refers to a qubit in which the pair of dots surrounded by exchange gate XA5 defines the $Z$ axis, and the pair of dots surrounded by exchange gate XA6 defines the $N$ axis.
L002679	With this notation established, a given set of three dots can equivalently be prepared in the $ZN$ or $NZ$ orientation, and two-qubit gates can be performed between any combination of two adjacent qubit orientations, given appropriate modifications to the pulse sequence.
L002680	It is straightforward to tile our 18-qubit device such that each qubit's $Z$ and $N$ axes only interact with the $Z$ and $N$ axes, respectively, of adjacent qubits.
L002681	For simplicity, this is our preferred configuration, and is used for all demonstrations in this work.
