L003014	The first two states use a circuit which first performs an $X$-stabilizer, then measures the final data qubits in the Z-basis (native to our architecture).
L003015	The last two states use a circuit which first performs a $Z$-stabilizer, then measures the final data qubits in the $X$-basis (by using a single-qubit $H$ gate before the measurement).
L003016	These states do not begin in a specific codespace, but rather a superposition of A and B (C) for the $Z$-basis ($X$-basis) initial states.
L003017	Therefore, the first stabilizer round will project the initial state into one of \emph{two} possible states, one in each of the possible codespaces.
L003018	For example, the initial state \(|0110\rangle\) will project either into state 4 or into state 8, depending on the ``sign'' assigned by the $X$-check (with an equal chance of either outcome).
L003019	
L003020	Without a flag qubit, the {[}{[}4,2,2{]}{]} code can detect any weight-1 event (i.e. single-qubit error) on the data qubits.
L003021	However, when a single $Z$ fault on the ancilla qubit occurs between the second and third entangler of either stabilizer, it propagates an error ($X$ or $Z$, depending on the stabilizer) onto two data qubits.
L003022	This changes the logical state, but keeps the total $Z$ or $X$ parity the same and is therefore an undetectable weight-1 error in our circuit.
L003023	To make our circuit robust to \emph{all} single qubit errors, we therefore add an additional ancilla, called the ``flag'', that entangles our original ancilla qubit around this dangerous location.
L003024	When a $Z$ error occurs on the ancilla halfway through a stabilizer round, the flag qubit will detect it, improving the logical fidelity of the circuit.
L003025	
L003026	To quantify the error-detecting capability of the {[}{[}4,2,2{]}{]} code, we post-select only the experiment shots where no error was detected.
L003027	Each such shot must meet four requirements:
L003028	
L003029	\begin{enumerate}
L003030		\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
L003031		\item
L003032		\emph{All flag measurements are} \(|0\rangle\)\emph{.}
L003033		\item
L003034		\emph{All $X$ syndrome measurements are identical and consistent with the targeted codespace.}
L003035		\item
L003036		\emph{All $Z$ syndrome measurements are identical and consistent with the targeted codespace.}
L003037		\item
L003038		\emph{The parity of the measured data qubits is consistent with the targeted codespace.}
L003039	\end{enumerate}
L003040	
L003041	For requirement 4, we use parities computed from data-qubit measurements to determine if an error occurred.
L003042	This is analogous to using the measurement of the data qubits in a repetition code to infer errors that occur during or after the last round of syndrome extraction.
L003043	
L003044	If any of the above checks fail, an error is considered to have occurred.
L003045	For example, if we started with the state $\ket{0000}$, the four requirements might be violated in the following ways:
L003046	
L003047	\begin{enumerate}
L003048		\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
L003049		\item
L003050		\emph{The flag measurement during the second $X$ stabilizer is triggered.}
L003051		\item
L003052		\emph{The first $X$ stabilizer is $-1$ and the second $X$ stabilizer is 1.}
L003053		\item
L003054		\emph{The first $Z$ stabilizer is $-1$  ($\ket{0000}$ must live in a $Z_{1234} = 1$ codespace).}
L003055		\item
L003056		\emph{The final measured state is $\ket{0001}$.}
L003057	\end{enumerate}
L003058	The ``circuit throughput'' is defined as the probability that a shot passes all of the post-selection rules.
L003059	
L003060	From these post-selected shots, we then check if the final measured state is in agreement with the state that was prepared, represented by two correct states.
L003061	For example, if we prepared the state $\ket{{-}{-}{+}{+}}$ both the state $\ket{{-}{-}{+}{+}}$ and $\ket{{+}{+}{-}{-}}$ are considered a success.
L003062	The ``circuit fidelity'' is then defined as the probability that a post-selected shot agrees with the prepared state.
L003063	Figure~\ref{figS52} summarizes the throughput and fidelity outcomes of all 10k shots of each prepared state.
L003064	Shots in which multiple post-selection rules were violated were only coarsely categorized, for figure simplicity.
L003065	
